      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785333664460,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How can we scale to support 1B shortened urls and 100M DAU?",
      "fontSize": 32.190910051358344,
      "fontFamily": 7,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How can we scale to support 1B shortened urls and 100M DAU?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.15
    },
    {
      "id": "Zha5Z77Porj2jQnPo4gr0",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 108.04412094671744,
      "y": 3570.27551830514,
      "width": 799.7193603515625,
      "height": 486,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1N",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1192244126,
      "version": 84,
      "versionNonce": 1360487902,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785333870511,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- To support 1 billion URLs, we estimate about 500 GB of storage, which a single\nPostgreSQL instance can handle. \n\n- Since reads are much more frequent than writes, we use Redis as a cache so most\nredirect requests never hit the database.\n\n- For high availability, PostgreSQL uses replication and backups. \n\n- We split the application into Read Services and Write Services so they can scale\nindependently behind a load balancer. Multiple Write Services share a central Redis\ncounter, using the atomic INCR operation to guarantee unique IDs. \n\n- To reduce Redis traffic, each Write Service requests a batch of IDs (for example, 1000\nat a time) and allocates them locally. \n\n- Redis can be made highly available using Sentinel or Cluster, and in a multi-region\ndeployment each region gets a non-overlapping ID range so that ID generation remains\nunique without requiring cross-region coordination.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- To support 1 billion URLs, we estimate about 500 GB of storage, which a single PostgreSQL instance can handle. \n\n- Since reads are much more frequent than writes, we use Redis as a cache so most redirect requests never hit the database.\n\n- For high availability, PostgreSQL uses replication and backups. \n\n- We split the application into Read Services and Write Services so they can scale independently behind a load balancer. Multiple Write Services share a central Redis counter, using the atomic INCR operation to guarantee unique IDs. \n\n- To reduce Redis traffic, each Write Service requests a batch of IDs (for example, 1000 at a time) and allocates them locally. \n\n- Redis can be made highly available using Sentinel or Cluster, and in a multi-region deployment each region gets a non-overlapping ID range so that ID generation remains unique without requiring cross-region coordination.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.35
    }
  ],
